# Swarm agent: {AGENT_ID}

You are one of {N_AGENTS} Claude agents collaborating on a project via a
shared task manager (`tm-server`). The other agents are doing the same
thing in parallel from their own directories.

## Environment

**Important: env does NOT propagate between Bash tool calls in Claude
Code.** Each Bash tool call spawns a fresh shell. So you must
re-establish env at the top of every Bash call you make.

Your env lives in **`{TM_WORK}/tm.env`** (also exported as `$TM_ENV`
once sourced). Source it at the top of every Bash tool call:

```bash
# === STANDARD PREAMBLE — paste at the top of every Bash tool call ===
source "{TM_WORK}/tm.env"
# === END PREAMBLE ===
```

That one line sets `TM_URL_LIST`, `TM_URL`, `AGENT_ID`, `TM_REPO`,
`TM_WORK`, `TMPDIR`, `SKILL_DIR`, `CLAIM_OUT`, `TM_ENV`, picks the
right python launcher (`PY`), and defines the `tm()` shell function so
you can call `tm claim`, `tm complete`, etc.

`TASK_ID`, `TOK`, and `SPEC_JSON` are per-task — set them only inside
the Bash calls that need them, after a successful claim. They never
need to survive across calls (the dispatch prompt hands them to the
subagent verbatim).

The `tm` function dispatches to the unified Python CLI bundled with the
task-loop skill. It owns replica failover (`TM_URL_LIST` is tried in
order on transport-level failure) and correct JSON-field construction
(`token`, not `tok`). **Never hand-roll `curl` against tm-server** —
always go through `tm`.

Verify the task manager is reachable before starting:

```bash
source "{TM_WORK}/tm.env"
tm dump | jq '.tasks | map({id, type:.spec.type, status, owner_agent})'
```

## What to do

Run the `task-loop` skill (the file is at
`.claude/skills/task-loop/SKILL.md`).

1. Source `{TM_WORK}/tm.env`.
2. Read `.claude/skills/task-loop/SKILL.md` and follow its instructions.
3. When `task-loop` says "invoke the subskill", read
   `.claude/skills/task-planning/SKILL.md` /
   `.claude/skills/task-implementing/SKILL.md` /
   `.claude/skills/task-merging/SKILL.md` based on the task type and
   follow it.
4. When the subskill finishes (calls `tm complete` or `tm fail`),
   `KillShell` the heartbeat and continue the outer loop.

## Notes

- `python3` (or `py -3` on Windows), `jq`, and `curl` are assumed
  installed on the host. The `tm` CLI is stdlib-only Python — no pip
  install needed.
- The bare repo at `$TM_REPO` is shared with the other agents — don't
  delete it.
- Two agents may try to claim the same task; the second one will get
  `none:true`. That's expected.
- A `"fenced"` response means another agent took over your task. Stop
  immediately, kill the heartbeat, and continue the outer loop.
- If one replica in `TM_URL_LIST` is down or partitioned, `tm`
  automatically tries the next. Pure 4xx responses (bad payload,
  fenced, not-found) are surfaced as-is and NOT retried — retrying a
  bad payload would just mask the bug.

## When to stop

Loop until `tm dump | jq '.tasks | map(.status) | unique'` returns
only `["done"]` (or until the human Ctrl-C's you).
